  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nStrong low-level warm air advection remains established across \nthe Midwest under a building upper-level ridge. Mid-level height\nrises and persistent south-southwesterly surface winds gusting \nup to 25 mph will push high temperatures into the upper 80s and\nlower 90s this afternoon, likely breaking daily high \ntemperature records. Unseasonably warm conditions persist \nthrough Tuesday ahead of an approaching upper-level trough. \nHighs on Tuesday again top out in the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nroughly 20 degrees above seasonal norms. Record highs remain \nsusceptible to being broken across central and southeast \nIllinois. High resolution guidance shows the front working into \nthe central CWA through Tuesday afternoon, and CAMs depict a few\npost-frontal showers/sprinkles over the northwest CWA. To\naccount for this, added some 20 PoPs for the Galesburg and\nPeoria areas.\n\nConvective initiation occurs Tuesday evening into Tuesday night\nas a lead shortwave tracks northeast toward the region. HREF\nguidance shows MUCAPE values building to 1000-1500 J/kg paired \nwith 0-6km bulk shear of 30-40 knots. ML/AI severe guidance, \nalong with the SPC Day 2 Outlook, maintain a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 of 5) for central and western portions of the forecast \narea. Primary convective hazards feature isolated severe wind \ngusts up to 60 mph and large hail up to quarter size.\n\nThe frontal boundary stalls in the area Wednesday into Thursday,\nbecoming oriented parallel to upper-level southwest flow as \nmore shortwave energy ejects from the trough to our west. Deep- \nlayer moisture surges along the boundary, with precipitable \nwater (PWAT) values reaching 1.75-2.00 inches, exceeding the \n95th percentile for mid-October. High-resolution ensemble \nforecasts (HREF) and medium-range guidance (ECMWF, GEFS, and \nECMWF-AI) support a few rounds of rain and embedded \nthunderstorms, with sizable breaks, Wednesday and Thursday. WPC \nexcessive rainfall outlooks highlight a Marginal Risk for heavy \nrain across central and southeast Illinois both days. Ensemble \nguidance (LREF) yields high confidence (60-80% chance) for event\ntotal rainfall exceeding 1 inch, with localized totals up to 2 \ninches. Some spread is noted in the footprint of heavier \nrainfall during this period, with recent guidance trending the \nhigher values to the northwest sections of the CWA.\n\nA sharp airmass change arrives Friday as an expansive Canadian \nsurface high pressure system builds into the Plains and Midwest.\nThe GFS/GEFS continues to be a cold outlier among the guidance \nsuite, showing temperatures several degrees colder than  the \npreferred NBM. Nonetheless, strong cold air advection on Friday \nwill keep highs below normal in the lower 60s, along with gusty \nnorthwest winds. The first frost of the season will be in play \nthis weekend, though this will likely hinge on the exact \nposition of the ridge axis. Confidence in the ridge axis being \noverhead during the early morning hours for calm winds and \nwidespread frost formation is low, but with the cold airmass in \nplace, lows in the 30s are likely both Saturday and Sunday \nmorning.",
